Summary: Composition not inheritance, polymorphism through interfaces, and packages for namespacing.
Keywords: golang, OOD, Object Oriented Design, Go, composition, interfaces

# Go Object Oriented Design

Go is a thoroughly object-oriented language. For example, it allows methods <mark>on any type</mark> we define, without all the boxing/unboxing common to other languages.

Go does **not** use classes, but provides many of the same features:

* automatic message delegation via <mark>embedding</mark>
* polymorphism via <mark>interfaces</mark>
* namespacing via <mark>exports</mark>

There is [no inheritance][composition] in Go. Forget about *is-a* relationships and think about OO design in terms of **composition**.

> "Use of classical inheritance is always optional; every problem that it solves can be solved another way." - [Sandi Metz][poodr]

## Composition by Example

Having recently [read][] through [Practical Object-Oriented Programming in Ruby][poodr], I decided to [translate][] the examples to Go.

Chapter 6 presents a simple problem. A mechanic needs to know which spare parts to bring on a bike trip, depending on which bikes have been rented. The problem can be solved using classical inheritance, where MountainBike and RoadBike are specializations of a Bicycle base class. Chapter 8 reworks the same example to <mark>use composition instead</mark>. I'm quite happy with how well this version [translated to Go][GitHub]. Let's take a look.

### Packages

```go
package main

import "fmt"
```

Packages provide a namespace. The `main()` function of the `main` package is where a program begins. The `fmt` package provides string formatting functions.


### Types

```go
type Part struct {
    Name        string
    Description string
    NeedsSpare  bool
}
```
 
We define a new type named Part. This structure is very much like a C struct.
 
```go
type Parts []Part
```

The Parts type is a *slice* of parts. Slices are dynamically resizable arrays, and are quite common in Go.

We can declare methods on any type, so we don't need to wrap `[]Part` in a struct. This means Parts can have <mark>all the behavior of slices, plus our own custom behavior.</mark>

### Methods

```go
func (parts Parts) Spares() (spares Parts) {
    for _, part := range parts {
        if part.NeedsSpare {
            spares = append(spares, part)
        }
    }
    return spares
}
```

A method declaration in Go is just like a function, except it has an <mark>explicit receiver</mark>, declared immediately after `func`. This function takes advantage of named return variables, pre-initializing spares for us.

The method body is fairly straightforward. We iterate over parts, ignoring the index position (`_`), and filtering the parts to return. The `append` [builtin][] mutates spares, but it may need to allocate and return a larger slice. 

This code isn't nearly as elegant as `select` in Ruby. A functional filter is possible in Go, it just isn't [builtin][].

### Embedding

```go
type Bicycle struct {
    Size string
    Parts
}
```

Bicycle is *composed of* a Size and Parts. By not specifying a named field for Parts, we make use of [embedding][]. This provides *automatic delegation* with no further ceremony, eg. `bike.Spares()` and `bike.Parts.Spares()` are equivalent.

It is possible to override `Spares()` on Bicycle and still reference the embedded `Parts.Spares()`. This *feels* almost like inheritance, but <mark>embedding does **not** provide polymorphism.</mark> The receiver of `Spares()` will always be of type Parts, even when called directly from a Bicycle. 

Patterns used with classical inheritance, like the template method pattern, aren't suitable for embedding. It's far better to think in terms of composition & delegation ([forwardable][] in *Ruby speak*), as we have here.


### Composite Literals

```go
var (
    RoadBikeParts = Parts{
        {"chain", "10-speed", true},
        {"tire_size", "23", true},
        {"tape_color", "red", true},
    }

    MountainBikeParts = Parts{
        {"chain", "10-speed", true},
        {"tire_size", "2.1", true},
        {"front_shock", "Manitou", false},
        {"rear_shock", "Fox", true},
    }

    RecumbentBikeParts = Parts{
        {"chain", "9-speed", true},
        {"tire_size", "28", true},
        {"flag", "tall and orange", true},
    }
)
```

Go provides a nice syntax for initializing objects, called [composite literals][]. Being able to initialize a struct with an array-like syntax made the PartsFactory from the Ruby example feel unnecessary.

```go
func main() {
    roadBike := Bicycle{Size: "L", Parts: RoadBikeParts}
    mountainBike := Bicycle{Size: "L", Parts: MountainBikeParts}
    recumbentBike := Bicycle{Size: "L", Parts: RecumbentBikeParts}
```

Composite literals can also use a `field: value` syntax, in which all fields are optional.

The short declaration operator (`:=`) uses <mark>type inference</mark> to initialize roadBike, etc. with the Bicycle type.

### Output

```go
    fmt.Println(roadBike.Spares())
    fmt.Println(mountainBike.Spares())
    fmt.Println(recumbentBike.Spares())
```

We print the the result of calling Spares in the default format: 

```
[{chain 10-speed true} {tire_size 23 true} {tape_color red true}]
[{chain 10-speed true} {tire_size 2.1 true} {rear_shock Fox true}]
[{chain 9-speed true} {tire_size 28 true} {flag tall and orange true}]
```

### Combining Parts

```go
    comboParts := Parts{}
    comboParts = append(comboParts, mountainBike.Parts...)
    comboParts = append(comboParts, roadBike.Parts...)
    comboParts = append(comboParts, recumbentBike.Parts...)

    fmt.Println(len(comboParts), comboParts[9:])
    fmt.Println(comboParts.Spares())
}
```

Parts behaves like a slice. Getting the length, taking a slice, or combining multiple slices all works as usual. The equivalent solution in Ruby is to subclass Array, but unfortunately Ruby "misplaces" the spares method when two Parts are concatenated.

> "...in a perfect object-oriented language this solution would be exactly correct. Unfortunately, the Ruby language has not quite achieved perfection..." - [Sandi Metz][poodr]

There is a somewhat ugly workaround in Ruby, using `Enumerable`, `forwardable`, and `def_delegators`. Go has no such deficiency. `[]Part` does exactly what we want, and it is far more concise.

## Interfaces

Polymorphism in Go is provided by interfaces. They are <mark>satisfied implicitly</mark>, unlike Java & C#, so interfaces can be defined for code we don't own.

Compared to <mark>duck typing</mark>, interfaces are statically checked and documented through their declaration, rather than through writing a series of `respond_to?` tests.

> "It is impossible to create an abstraction unknowingly or by accident; in statically typed languages defining an interface is _always_ intentional." - [Sandi Metz][poodr]

For a simple example, let's say we didn't want to print the NeedsSpare flag of Part. We could write a String method as such:

```go
func (part Part) String() string {
    return fmt.Sprintf("%s: %s", part.Name, part.Description)
}
```

Then the calls to `Println` above would output this instead:

```
[chain: 10-speed tire_size: 23 tape_color: red]
[chain: 10-speed tire_size: 2.1 rear_shock: Fox]
[chain: 9-speed tire_size: 28 flag: tall and orange]
```

This works because we have satisfied the Stringer interface, which the `fmt` package makes use of. It is defined as:

```go
type Stringer interface {
    String() string
}
```

Interface types can be used in the same places as other types. Variables and arguments can take a Stringer, which accepts anything that implements the `String() string` method signature.

## Exports

Go uses packages for namespacing. Identifiers that begin with a capital letter are exported. To make an identifier "private" to a package, we just start it with a lowercase letter.

```go
type Part struct {
    name        string
    description string
    needsSpare  bool
}
```

To apply the <mark>uniform access principle</mark> to the Part type, we could change the struct definition and provide setter/getter methods such as:

```go
func (part Part) Name() string {
    return part.name
}

func (part *Part) SetName(name string) {
    part.name = name
}
```

It's easy to determine what is using the public API vs. internal fields or methods. Just look at the case of the identifier (eg. `part.Name()` vs. `part.name`).

Notice that we don't prefix [getters][] with Get (eg. `GetName`). Getters aren't strictly necessary either, especially with strings. When the need arises, we can always change the Name field to use a custom type that satisfies the Stringer interface.

### Finding Some Privacy

Private names (lowerCase) can be accessed from anywhere in the same package, even if the package contains multiple structs across multiple files. If you find this unsettling, packages can also be as small as you need.

It is good practice to use the *(more stable)* public API when possible, even from within the same class in classical languages. This requires convention, which we can certainly apply in Go.

Alternatively, Go provides first-class functions and closures, which are commonly used for namespacing in languages like JavaScript.

## For Great GOOD

Composition, embedding and interfaces provide powerful tools for Object-Oriented Design in Go. Thinking in terms of inheritance *really* doesn't work. Trust me, [I tried][translate].

While Idiomatic Go requires a change in thinking, I am pleasantly surprised with how simple & concise Go code can be, when playing to the strengths of Go's object model.


